{"identity" {"database" "math" "lineage" "01a12a15-3e0a-75a1-9dac-f8b8c5d5c3f1" "basis" 3234} "tutorial" [[{:db/id 17592186075064 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe showed that the set of intersection points $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) = \\left(x,3x + 2\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number.\n\nThis formula allows us to generate as many intersection points as we want! To do this, we select *any* $x$-value and substitute it into the formula above.\n\nFor example:\n\n- If we set $x=0$, we get the point\n$(x, y) = (0, 3(0) + 2) = (0, 2)$.\nThis point is indeed a solution to the system:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3(0) - 2 &=-2 \\\\ 6(0) - 2(2) &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(0,2)$ is a solution.\n- If $x= 1$, we get the point\n$(x, y) = (1, 3(1) + 2) = (1, 5)$.\nThis point is another solution to the system:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3(1) - 5 &=-2 \\\\ 6(1) - 2(5) &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(1,5)$ is a solution.\n\nBy selecting any $x$-value and finding the corresponding $y$-value, $(x,y)$ gives a solution to the system and, consequently, a point of intersection between the two lines." :tutorial/id #uuid "1b845842-4e57-5e13-88c4-5f2040525075" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9686 :tutorial/title "Understanding the Result"}]] "incoming" [[17592186074712 1063] [17592186574771 1158]] "parents" [[{:db/id 17592186074712 :step/content {:db/id 17592186075064} :step/id #uuid "c5a54d4e-6187-547b-a529-49836ff17503" :step/math-academy-id 229018 :step/next {:db/id 17592186074713}}] [{:db/id 17592186574771 :task-item/content {:db/id 17592186075064} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 58.82 :task-item/id #uuid "6eb67473-9388-460a-8ced-97e7ee68d364" :task-item/next {:db/id 17592186574772} :task-item/status {:db/id 17592186045570}}]]}
